In article <33162B5F.437B@silicon.net.my>
           fskok@silicon.net.my "Kok Fock Seng" writes:

>Hi out there,
>
>I am writing a program utilize the message queue (IPC) feature
>in Dec Unix. 

Note that your question was not about the C language so it wasnt' really
appropriate to cross-post to comp.lang.c. (Removed from follow-ups)

>void main ()

Since you have cross-posted to comp.lang.c I'd just point out that C defines
main as a function returning int. More specifically as either:

int main(void)

or

int main(int argc, char *argv[])

>{
>  int   messageid;
>
> m.msgtype = 1L ;
> strcpy(m.msgbuf,"This is a test msg!") ;
>
> if  (( messageid = msgget ((key_t)(6000L), IPC_CREAT)) == -1 ) 

(pretending I'm reading this in comp.unix.programmer)

There is a problem here in that you failed to give any read/write
permissions on the queue. Check your man page more carefully for what
the 2nd argument should be (it is basically a collection of bit flags
similar to the 2nd argument of open())

>        exit (-1);

Why -1? C defines 0, EXIT_SUCCESS and EXIT_FAILURE. Unix essentially
takes the lower order 8 bits of the value (i.e. a value 0-255) although
some shells may have a weirdness of treating this like a signed char.

> fprintf(stderr, "[%d]", messageid);
>
>  if ( msgsnd ( messageid, &m, strlen(m.msgbuf)+1, 0) == -1)
>        fprintf(stderr, "Test Queue got problem!\n err [%d]", errno);
>  else fprintf(stderr, "Test Queue OK!\n");
>  printf(stderr, "[%d]\n", errno) ;

You need to make up your mind whether to printf this to stdout or
fprintf it to stderr! :-)

