In comp.unix.admin Yijia Sun <608139226@worldnet.att.net> wrote:
: Can any one tell me how to setup restrictive finger service system wise or
: user bases...

Two options -- one is to use the GNU fingerd package which you can
fetch from ftp.cdrom.com in /pub/gnu or you can just write your
own little fingerd. Either way, you need to go into your /etc/inetd.conf
and set it to run your fingerd instead of the default one. Here is a
sample fingerd I used at a site I admined remotely for a while -- it's
not fancy but I'd like to think I have more important things to do than
optimize fingerd. =)

(btw... javabooks.net is no longer on the net, for those who may be curious...)


#!/usr/local/bin/perl

print << "ENDOFHEADER";
_______________________________________________________________________________

                          Welcome to Java Books Cafe

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


ENDOFHEADER

$request = <STDIN>;
chop $request;
chop $request;

if ($request eq "") {
        print "For additional information about Java Books Cafe, visit\n";
        print "our homepage at http://www.javabooks.net\n";
        print "\n\n\n";
        exit (0);
}

if ($request eq "uptime") {
        open (UPTIME,"uptime|") || die "Cannot read uptime.\n";
        $info = <UPTIME>;
        close (UPTIME);
        print "$info\n\n\n\n";
        exit (0);
}


if ($request eq "gbhp") {
        print "The Greg Bulmash Humor Page has moved to www.bulmash.com.\n";
        print "\n\n";
        exit (0);
}


print "If $request has a homepage here, you may want to try\n";
print "accessing it at http://www.javabooks.net/~$request\n";
print "\n\n";


-- 
