This is a big post (sorry!), and you would need patience to read it
through and understand my questions.

If anyone out there has a good understanding of the Linux kernel sources
concerning the implementation of the various types of file system (fs),
I would GREATLY appreciate your advice. In fact I'm not that bothered if
your knowledge of the kernel if gained from other UNIX platforms, I'm
only interested in the principles of operation. These questions relate
to a project I'm doing with the DEC Alpha, running Digital UNIX, but as
always I first turn to Linux to understand how things work, before
looking at the details and differences of Digital UNIX.

WHAT I BASICALLY WANT TO KNOW IS: 
What parts of the kernel would need to be changed to make Linux
understand and work with a new proprietary file system format, other
than the ones already supported, such as umsdos and ex2fs?

Say you had a PC with a SCSI host bus adapter and a SCSI hard disk and
you want to run Linux? In such a case you must install a version of
Linux with a kernel that supports the SCSI host bus adapter card of your
system - Right? You could then make Linux work with a SCSI hard disk of
any manufacturer - atleast in theory. So as long as the kernel has a
device driver for your SCSI card, and support for the file system type
you want, everthing should be fine. Am I correct in my understanding of
this?

Now with SCSI, as you know you can daisy chain up to 7 other devices. So
suppose you wanted to have another SCSI hard disk in your system. A new
device driver is not required surely?. You basically connect a cable to
the new disk and the previous disk, may be set some jumpers and take
care of mount points, making a new filesystem, and some other
administrative details with /etc/fstab, and AWAY YOU GO - Another hard
disc in your system ready for use. Am I correct so far?

NOW THIS IS THE TWIST AND WHAT I NEED ADVICE WITH: Suppose that the
second SCSI hard disk is not some off the shelf product that you see in
the computer shops and magazines. Suppose that it is an EXTERNAL
RACKMOUNTED SCSI UNIT WITH NOT JUST A SCSI BUS CONNECTION, BUT ALSO
ANOTHER PROPIETARY BUS CONNECTOR of massively greater bandwidth than
SCSI, used in the real time telemetry and data acquisition industries.
This disk acts as a 'fast data recorder'; it is fed with massive
quantities of raw data from this other bus (from other rack mounted
electronics such as analogue to digital converter modules). When the
disk is recording data it dumps the raw bytes in a proprietary file
format, defined by the manufacturer of that disk. When the disk is
recording data it is not accessed by the host (the PC), but when it is
finished, you effectively have files on the second disk in an alien file
system format to those that Linux recognizes. That disk can accept
commands from the other bus, to allow it to do basic administration,
such as formating it in some proprietary way.

IF I HAD THE SPECIFICATION OF THAT PROPRIETARY FILE SYSTEM FORMAT, ie.
if I could get the details of how the blocks on the disks are mapped to
files, (I think it is a flat file system with no directories), how much
work is involved to get Linux to recogize that file system? By
recognize, all I want Linux to do, is to allow a user to list the files
of that disk so that they just look like UNIX files (with some sensible
defaults for the access permissions), and allow a user program to use
the normal open(), read(), write().... calls on them.
So what I am trying to do is to make a foreign file system format look
like a normal UNIX directory of files.
In terms of design issues isn't this analogous to the design of the
UMSDOS file system for LINUX?

My thinking is that the SCSI device driver for the adapter card could
remain the same, but a new device driver for the foreign file system
would have to be written. The user would merely mount the disk using a
device special file representing the driver for the foreign filesystem
format. The command to make a file system would probably not be needed,
as this would be issued by the other rack mounted electronics to create
the proprietary format, prior to using the disk for data recording. So
when data recording was finished, a nice looking UNIX GUI program could
do read() and write()s with those telemetry files and display the data
in fancy graphs and plots etc. How much work is involved in writing such
a device driver? It is surely not a SCSI device driver that's required,
but something at a slightly higher level of abstraction? I understand
that it depends on how easy it is to interpret the proprietary file
format, but say that was easy and nicely defined,
how much new source needs to be written, and which existing functions of
the kernel would need work? Please give me as much details as you can.

IF NOBODY REPLIES TO THIS POST,(apart from not being suprised, and very
disappointed) I will just plod along, studying the kernel sources of
Linus, the LINUX KERNEL HACKERS GUIDE and reading the DIGITAL UNIX
online books on SCSI block device drivers. Eventually I will know enough
to know how to do it, but if someone gave me some pointers; that could
save me days, or weeks even; so please help. Thanks for reading so far!
and even greater thanks if you reply.



