Mark Taylor wrote:
> 
> On 18 Feb 1997 17:47:34 -0500 Brian 'Doc' O'Neill (oneill@cs.uml.edu) wrote:
> >Does anybody know why the /bin/csh that comes with 4.0B kicks people out for
> >simple syntax errors in their .cshrc or .login files? This has caused a LOT
> >of trouble for me, as I'm constantly getting phone calls from people who
> >can't log in...
> >

If the shell exits, then the person will be logged out. This is
pretty much immutable (-:

> 
> The /bin/csh which comes with Digital Unix 4.0 is buggy.

Would't surprise me.

> 
> The bugs I have come across are to do with pattern matching, and
> are quite serious (csh can hang just trying to do a pattern match,
> as well as giving some *very* odd results).

I would like to see some examples. (Out of curiosity - I am no
longer in a position to actually fix things)

> The bugs were introduced apparently when they rewrote csh for POSIX
> compatibility. 

Csh is not Posix compliant, as there is no Posix csh defined. 
DEC may very well have made some changes to csh (and I can guess
who the engineer probably was) , but there is no standards body
that defines csh. Thus the changes were not for Posix , but were
probably bug fixes.

> The fix I have used is just taking the /bin/csh binary
> from Digital Unix 3.2 and using that instead, it seems to work
> perfectly OK but apparently isn't POSIX compliant, but I can live
> with that.

I actually worked on csh for DEC, cleaning up the 8 bit quoting and
fixing the endless recursion logout , etc.  But I think that there
might have been a major rewrite later. The csh code is not very good
and the lack of a standard definition for behavior doesn't help.

> 
> You could try getting Digital to rewrite the csh, but when I reported
> the bug the man on the end of the phone asked me nicely not to,
> and I figured it wouldn't be a very fast solution, so I didn't
> push it.
> 
> I'm not certain this is the same problem you're seeing of course ...
> 
> I can get you a 3.2 /bin/csh binary if you want.
> 

My personal recommendation is to not use csh. Go for the standard Posix
sh, which is ksh. 


Charlie

P.S. The "bad code" that I refer to , predates any DEC changes.

