"Andrew" == Andrew Kuan <andykuan@newscorp.com> writes:

Andrew> I've got a doozy . . . I have a pair of servers that have been
Andrew> DECsafed together.  They are running an NFS service.  When
Andrew> changes are made to the NFS service's exports file, DECSafe
Andrew> has to delete the service and then add it back in again with
Andrew> the changes in place.

Andrew> Problem is, whenever I do this, DECsafe goes ahead and chews
Andrew> up my routing tables.  Here's a snapshot:

[...]

If you read the manual, it suggests putting in static routes to each
interface of each member of your /etc/routes file, like so...

default 130.194.11.254
-host foo foo
-host bar bar
-host foo-local foo-local
-host bar-local bar-local

(XYZ-local is the interface for the local net i have between the two
machines)

This approach seems to work fine. No gated, no mess, no fuss :)

If you pull out the "rest of world" ethernet, it'll change the routing
tables to use the backup link, but as long as you only tell ASE to
monitor the rest-of-world interface and not the "local-net" one, i'll
move the service over to the other member that can still see the
world.

ie. :

                ASE Network Configuration

    Member Name          Interface Name       Member Net   Monitor   
    ___________          ______________       __________   _______   

    bar                bar                Backup       Yes       
    bar                bar-local          Primary      No        

    foo                 foo                 Backup       Yes       
    foo                 foo-local           Primary      No        


Enjoy,
 Andrew.
