I had the same problem, and I managed to get it working in the
following way:  There are two sections in devices.c that are for OSF
machines.  One uses device /dev/rfd0c, and one uses device /dev/fd0a.
I took out the first block, which was used by default, so it would
take the second one.  Using /dev/fd0a, everything has worked
perfectly.  Incidentally, I'm using mtools-3.1 instead of 3.0.

Now a warning:  I have no idea why this worked for me.  I'm not sure
what the difference in the device names is exactly.  Looking at the
directory entries, /dev/rfd0c is a character device and /dev/fd0a is a
block device.  It makes more sense to me that mtools would want to use
a block device (you can't seek on a character device, can you?).  I'm
not sure what the difference between the "a" and the "c" is...

