I've spent quite a bit of time performance tuning Networker, so maybe I
can help. I must disagree with something another poster said - that
NSR's overhead isn't much more than dd. I'm running on a fairly old
Alpha 3000/400, writing to just one DLT2000, and I believe that the
processor isn't capable of doing much more. Try looking at the load of
the machine when you're doing backups. More specifically, don't look at
the load, but use top or syd or similar and look at the CPU idle time.
If there isn't much, and the CPU is nearly always in system mode, then
this explains your performance problem. (This is my situation).

What drives are you using that can write at 2.5Mb/sec? The DLT2000 is
capable of about 1.2Mb and the DLT400 about 1.5Mb...

What is the load on your SCSI bus like? What sort of SCSI is it?

There are different tests you can try to see where the bottleneck is,
but the list to test is: tape speed, SCSI speed, processor usage,
network usage, client usage. I've also found that in high-parallelism
situations, the size of the save-sets can make a big difference since a
small number of large save-sets will tend to be badly scheduled by NSR.

> Also, the index files under /nsr/index are much larger than I would
> have expected them to be.  Is there a worksheet or something somewhere
> for calculating how much disk space we should need for them?

I seem to remember that somewhere amongst the numerous help files it
says that about 100 bytes are required per file per backup. It is quite
a lot, and is sure does add up (I have about 10 Gb of space for indexes
and am still running out).

Cheers,

Matt
