Machine: DEC alpha 3000 300/300L AXP
OS: Digital Unix 4.0

In the headerfile /usr/include/values.h
are all the max-min-values defined!

I was surprised to see that the values of

MINFLOAT and MINDOUBLE

are defined less then the compiler can work with!
First I thought that it is a problem with the parser
but it is not.
Then I compared the cxx and cc and it is with both the same!

float f1 = 1.4e-38;  // is ok
float f2 = f1/10;    // is NOT ok!
float f3 = f2*10;    // is still NOT ok!

cout and printf gave the same results for f1,f2,f3:
float f1:   1.4013e-38
float f2:   0
float f3:   0

MINFLOAT is defined as 1.40129846432481707e-45!!!
This is the real minimun with the denormalized form.

Is it true that DEC does not support this but the 
minimum is defined as this?
It is pretty amazing.

With MAXFLOAT and MAXDOUBLE everything is ok.

Does somebody have work-arounds or patches?

Remark: On a Sun UltraSparc with Solaris 2.5 everything is 
        ok with CC and cc!

Armin



