We have two 4100s and a 2100, plus roughly 20GB of data to back up from our
disk farm, soon to be 50GB.  We need to back up the whole mess (no
incrementals) every night, two copies, in about seven hours.  The 2100 acts
as the server, currently with one TZ877, soon to be six. We're using NSR to
run everything, BUT...

 1) NSR's "cloning" feature is crippled -- it waits until the first tape is
written, and then copies it.  We thought that the copies were written in
parallel...is this possible?  If we have to wait until the first copy is
finished, our backup window is effectively halved.

 2) NSR multiplexes data from several clients onto one tape, which is cool
for speed, but not for disaster recovery.  If there's a bad spot on the
tape, we lose our backup of not just one client, but all clients that were
backing up at the time.

 3) The jukebox licensing policy basically sucks.  Regardless of how few
clients you're backing up, you get popped $5000 for each jukebox you set
up.  The TZ877s only cost $6000!  In our case, with six TZ877s, we'd have
to cough up $30,000.  Not bloody likely.

So we're not real happy with NSR, and we're trying to come up with
alternatives.  Using vdump/vrestore would be fine with me, but then we get
no tape library management, which will make the operators and users very
unhappy.

Any suggestions would be appreciated (and failing that, some sympathy would
be nice... :-)

-daniel

