Allen M. Cohen wrote:
> 
> Tim Cochran wrote:
> >
> > Hello,
> >
> > I am compiling and running a program in C++ (gcc 2.7.2) under Digital UNIX
> > 3.2c.  When I run the program I get Unaligned access errors.  The program
> > does not crash, just print out these errors.  Does anyone know what this
> > means?  (Other programs I have written don't have this problem.)
> >
> > Thanks in advance,
> > Tim
> You need to step trough the program in gdb.

That answer is, while correct, a bit discouraging if the program in
question consists of some 500,000+ source lines.

You can use the information from the error message a bit more creatively
in gdb, I'll show an example:

The message looks like this:
Unaligned access pid=29382 <name> va=143703f7 pc=12130abc ra=12130a84
type=ldl

The most interesting address is the "pc", this is the program counter
where the error occurs. You set a breakpoint at this address like this
in gdb:

(gdb) b *0x12130abc
Breakpoint 1 at 0x12130abc: file buggy_file.c, line 753.

As you see, this immediately pin-pointed a code line. You won't always
be
that lucky but you can always run the program and it will stop at the
breakpoint. 

Im my experience, unaligned access messages always indicate something
seriously
wrong with the program (typically pointer arithmetics and/or casting
problems)
and should always be taken seriously.

hope this helps,

Roland Kruse



