What, exactly, are you trying to do? Precise examples are of much more
use than ambiguous English descriptions. I can only guess that you're
doing something wrong -- I've got no idea what that might be. The best
description of a problem is a full source file that someone can compile
to reproduce the behavior. Code fragments leave the possibility of
errors outside the fragment. And show how you're BUILDING it, too. You
should never require the person trying to help you to GUESS at anything,
because that impairs their ability to assist you. It may, in fact,
cause them to decide it's not worth their time to even try.

Also, it's always a bad idea to start by trying to diagnose a problem
("unable to handle an address argument") rather than describing what
you're doing and what you see as a result. You'd be embarrassed if you
took a car with a thumping noise into a shop and told them that the
transmission needed to be replaced -- only to find (maybe after a few
thousand in repair bills) that the noise was just the rear hatch, which
you had forgotten to close.

