From: lenox@zso.dec.com (Amy Lenox)
Subject: Re: NSR/Legato problems
Date: 1997/02/05
Message-ID: <5daftk$c5i@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 214648352
references: <32F76624.39B4@isc.upenn.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation - DECwest Engineering
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In article <32F76624.39B4@isc.upenn.edu> msirota@isc.upenn.edu writes:
>We're using Digital's Network Save and Restore, aka Legato Networker,
                       ^^^^^^^ NetWorker
>to back up 16 client to a 20/40 DLT jukebox with two drives.  The
>backups are running on an AlphaServer 1000A which is dedicated to the
>task, and the backups are done over a 100-megabit/second ethernet, also
>dedicated to the task.

You don't say what type of memory you have on the system, what type
of load there is on the network, what type of client configuration
(all clients have few/many disks, 16 8200s, whatever), if you are trying
to save the server at the exact same time as the clients, if the tape
drives are on the same scsi bus, etc...  There are so many things which
contribute to NetWorker performance, it isn't something one can write up
in a few paragraphs.  Hopefully the below will give you some idea.  Do not
expect to see complete utilization of your network, either in all the
stuff below, 12.5MB/s is not realistic traffic.

>The tape drives are capable of writing at 2.5 meg/second, according to
>the test in the manual which uses dd to write a big file to the tape
>drive.  Yet while backing up, nwadmin shows much lower figures -- it
>generally starts at about 1.0MB/s, and deteriorates as the backup
>proceeds -- towards the end, it's generally under 200KB/s.

dd is not the same as NetWorker.  NetWorker does add some overhead
to what is written on tape and it does index what is written to tape.
Not a lot of overhead, it is just doing something that dd doesn't do,
so comparison is not equivalent.  Some people do see close to 2.5 MB/s
on multiple drives.  They tend to have bigger machines than you with
lots of memory and have tried various configurations to see what 
works best in their environment.  Toward the end of a backup there
are few sessions coming from clients, so the ability of the clients
to send data to the server is a limiting factor.  There are fewer
sessions streaming to tape so there probably will be lower performance. 

>We've got the server parallelism turned all the way up (32), and have
>sessions/device set to 8.  Lower values, particularly for parallelism,
>definitely seem to hurt.  What can we do to improve tape performance?
>Why is it so low?  Why does it deteriorate so?

Did you start with parallelism high?  That probably isn't a good thing
to set parallelism at for the server (unless you had a large 8400 to start
with).  Are you seeing that there are usually 8 sessions per device
(seems high to me too, but..).  A good way to start tuning a system is
to try varying the server parallelism value and set the sessions per device
low (say 2).  This will insure that the sessions load balance on the devices
(since they will start accepting more sessions if the parallelism of the
server has not been reached).  This can improve the streaming of the tape
drives.  If your network is clogged, try doing compression on the clients,
otherwise compression at the tape drive is best or try distributing the
full saves over the period of a week or month so only a few fulls are 
done on any particular day.  If you save the server at the same time as
the clients, try saving the server later, since it will slow the server
down (I/O wise).

>Also, the index files under /nsr/index are much larger than I would
>have expected them to be.  Is there a worksheet or something somewhere
>for calculating how much disk space we should need for them?

The size of the index files depends on the number of files you will
be keeping track of.  Frequently people leave browse and retention
periods to the defaults, when they don't need them to be that long.
Reduce the browse period to what you really want and eventually you
should see a reduction in the size of the index.  The Addendum (shipped
with the product) should have various suggestions for using NetWorker.
The postscript docs will be put on the server system if the release
notes subset is installed. 

>Lastly, under the "Details" view for Client Setup, there are two (or
>more) undocumented variables that seem like they might be useful,
>called "Priority" (default 500, range 1-1000) and "Parallelism" (default
>8).  What are these?  Where are they documented?  Might they help?

I doubt they will help.  Both affect how client saving is organized.
E.g. a maximum of 8 save sessions from a client are allowed (this
is generally more than one will see unless one has many disks, etc...).

>Thanks in advance for any tips you can provide.
>-- 
>Mark Sirota, Network Systems Engineer
>University of Pennsylvania, Information Systems and Computing
>msirota@isc.upenn.edu, 215/573-7214

regards,

A. Lenox
lenox@zso.dec.com
