x-mailer: Mozilla 2.01E-NC260  (Win16; I)


The disktab entry for the RZ28 family states that the quoted parameters 
are "approximate" as the number of tracks per cylinder depends on the 
position on the disk.

When creating ufs file systems with newfs, if the partition size doesn't 
start or end on a multiple of the apparently "artificial" "sectors per 
cylinder" value, then a message like "xx MB in last cylinder unallocated" 
is often seen. This presumably because newfs feels constrained to build 
filesystems starting/ending on its idea of a cylinder boundary. (I recall 
a Sun system that refused to even consider creating a filesystem unless 
the partition started on an even cylinder boundary.)

The question(s): does the physical geometry of an RZ28 have any bearing 
on how (ufs) filesystems can or should be built on it, beyond the obvious 
like maximum size. Is newfs slavishly following unnecessary logic in 
shunning parts of a partition on an RZ28, when disktab suggests that 
dividing "number of sectors" by the quoted "sectors per cylinder" doesn't 
actually reveal anything about the location of sectors on the disk wrt 
physical cylinder boundaries? What's going on, if anything?

Yours in ignorance.



