This doesn't mean Legato is a good backup solution. I think OpenVision satisfies 
the above. Certainly far more has to be considered when deciding on what backup 
tool to use. Among them are the following:
	1. How quickly after a new OS version do they have a client available?
	2. Does it do throttling?
	3. If you have a DLT library with multiple SCSI busses, can you connect
	   multiple servers to the library and have them back up in parallel?
	4. Can you do load-balancing of backups to multiple backup masters?
	5. How simple is restoring a full backup?
	6. What format does the software use when writing the tapes?
	7. Does it support multiple masters and/or backup masters?
	8. Can you create duplicate tapes (onsite and offsite storage) during
	   the day amongst DLT libraries (or DLT and other storage technologies)?
	9. How easy is it to manage multiple backup templates (defines what to
	   back up)?
	10. Does the software require .rhosts?
	12. ...

In addition to the above, I would suggest reading "Torture-testing Backup and 
Archive Programs: Things you ought to know but probably would rather not", 
Proceedings of the Fifth Large Installation Systems Administration Conference 
(LISA V), pp. 181-189, Elizabeth D. Zwicky.

