[ Roland Kruse ]

>>  Im my experience, unaligned access messages always indicate something
>>  seriously
>>  wrong with the program (typically pointer arithmetics and/or casting
>>  problems)
>>  and should always be taken seriously.

Well, I don't think I'd go *that* far ...  Something as trivial as

	real*4 f
	real*8 d
	common /spam/ f, d

will cause an unaligned access whenever d is referenced.  One can argue
that it might be sloppy programming practice so arrange Fortran common
blocks or C structs so that things aren't on "natural" boundaries, but
I wouldn't call it "seriously wrong".  Still worth fixing, of course,
particularly in view of the performance hit that would result of there
are a lot of unaligned access traps.

