In article <32F9A1FB.1858@netcomuk.co.uk>
           jhall@netcomuk.co.uk "john hall" writes:

> The question(s): does the physical geometry of an RZ28 have any bearing 
> on how (ufs) filesystems can or should be built on it

You probably already know this .... but
The RZ28 and above use zoned recording. This is to increase the 
capacity of the disk. For example say the surface can safely record
a sector in 1 unit length of track. On the innermost track this gives
n sectors. The outermost track ( much longer) may be able to hold 2n
sectors at the same density. If all tracks were limited to having
the same number of sectors a large amount of disk space would be 
wasted. AFAIK the on board disk controllers can't report this subtlty
of the disk geometry. Therefore newfs ( which in an interface to mkfs)
can't know about this. Also the algorithms used predate the widespread
use of zoned disks.

The new wisdom is that alligning on cylinder boundries is no longer
necessary ( which is just as well because there is no easy way of 
doing it :-) )



