>>>>> "Joe" == Joe Spanicek <joe@resptk.bhp.com.au> writes:

Joe>  Simon H. P. Fung wrote:

Simon>   Could someone show me how to setup my Digital UNIX 4.0 box to
Simon>  print to a HP printer which is connected to the LAN via a HP
Simon>  JetDirect print server?
Simon>  

Joe>  This is my printcap entry: I set it up using the GUI Printer Setup
Joe>  in DU4.0

Joe>  # HP1600CM Color Postscript DeskJet printer 
Joe>  lp1|1|color:\
Joe>          :lf=/usr/adm/lp1err:\ 
Joe>          :lp=:\ 
Joe>          :mx#0:\ 
Joe>          :rm=qmpcolor:\ 
Joe>          :rp=RAW:\
Joe>          :sd=/usr/spool/lpd1:\ 
Joe>          :sh:

Joe>  You may want to try 'rp=RAW' - the printer should be able to
Joe>  distinguish whether it is plain text or postscript.

  It would be nice if the printer distinguished between plain text and
  postscript; unfortunately, it doesn't.  If you print a text file to
  the raw queue on the printer, it ignores carriage returns, and you get
  a nice staircase effect with the lines marching off to the right of
  your page.

  One way around this problem is to set up two queues, one for text,
  with rp=text in the printcap entry, and the other for everything else
  (postscript, HPGL, PCL) with rp=raw.  The user then has to remember to
  point text at the text queue and everything else at the raw queue.

  Another way around it is to set up a filter on the host and turn the
  text into postscript before firing it at the printer.  Then you only
  need one printcap entry for rp=raw.  Don't try to use filtering in the
  printcap entry though, as it disregards filtering for remote queues.
  Instead, put a small wrapper around whatever commands you use to print
  (lpr, lp...), or just pass it through a filter before calling lpr:

     /usr/lbin/ln03rof< filename | lpr 

  Good luck,
		Phil
