From: Dirk Grunwald <grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>
Subject: Re: Help! One file system over 2 disks?
Date: 1997/02/02
Message-ID: <qxpybd6tu6m.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 214077579
references: <32F2D81D.436F@avana.net>
organization: University of Colorado
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



There are two ways to do this.

First, you can use the logical storage manage (LSM), which is similar
to the logical volume manager (LVM), but LVM is being phased out. See
"man volintro" for details.  Once you use the LSM utilities to
aggregate your disks, you can lay down any kind of file system you
like.  Unhappily, I think that DEC is now charging for LSM support or
some such.

Alternatively or in addition, you can use the advanced file system
(ADVFS). This is a an alternative to the UFS file system normally
used. ADVFS is a log-structured file system and has many nice
features. See "man advfs" for details.

Basically, advfs lets you aggregate a number of disks into a "file
domain". You can then carve one or more "file sets" from the "file
domain". The file sets compete with each other for space in the file
domain. You can add and remove volumes dynamicaly and stripe
individual files.

However, I think you need the "addvol" command to do what you'd like.
Again, this is from the licensed part of the software world. The basic
AdvFS stuff is free, but you pays for the good stuff.

The ADVFS file system has the distinct advantage of not needing an
"fsck" on system bootup. If you're using a system with lots of disk,
this is essential following crashes/power outages/etc.

-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



