Subject: Swapon - Deferred vs. immediate swapping
Date: 1997/02/03
Message-ID: <01bc11f5$93abf0b0$f9ef1bac@usbgrjeff>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 214157695
organization: Landis & Staefa
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


We have a Alpha-2100 5/250 (2 processors) with 1 GB of Ram.  We were using
the default immediate swapping method that seemed to eat up tremendous
amounts of swap file space (> 1GB).  Memory always seemed to be used up -
it always seemed to be around 400 pages free.  We seemed to have problems
with the machine appearing to stall for a couple of seconds every now and
then.  While typing the cursor would freeze and then let go...and CPU usage
didn't appear to be a factor.  It even did this on the console so the IP
network was eliminated.

A DEC friend of ours told us that we should probably use the Deferred
swapping methodology.  We tried it and things appear to be much better.  We
seem to have plenty of free memory and our swap file usage is 0%.

What swapping method do other large memory UNIX alpha servers use?  Were
still running UNIX 3.2c with a ton of Oracle financial databases.  There
probably is about 400+ processes running on this machine via a ps -ef
command.  Also have HSZ-40 with 32 MB write-back cache.

